@page "/components/ai/artifactview"
@implements IAsyncDisposable

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/artifactview"
            Title="AiArtifactView"
            Description="BitAiArtifactView is the panel an AI artifact lives in - code, a document, a sandboxed HTML page, JSON, a table or an image - with its versions, copy, download and full screen." />

<DemoPage Name="AiArtifactView"
          SecondaryNames="@(["Canvas"])"
          Description="BitAiArtifactView is the panel an artifact lives in: the substantial piece of content an assistant produces beside its answer - a program, a document, a page, a table, an image - and revises over the turns that follow. The header holds the title, a version selector once there is more than one version, copy, download and a full-screen toggle; the body is chosen by the kind of the artifact - a renderer the app registered, or a built-in one: highlighted code, a streaming Markdown document, an HTML page in a sandboxed frame, a JSON tree, a table, or an image. The panel follows the artifact as it streams, and copy and download act on the version on screen."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="ArtifactView/BitAiArtifactView.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/ArtifactView/BitAiArtifactViewDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            BitAiArtifactView reads the artifact renderers registered with <b>AddBitBlazorUIAIServices</b>
            (<b>ConfigureRenderers</b>), so the app calls it at startup even where it registers none, and downloads through the
            scripts of Bit.BlazorUI.AI. An artifact belongs to a conversation: a session creates it from the
            <b>BitAiArtifactCreatedEvent</b>, <b>BitAiArtifactDeltaEvent</b> and <b>BitAiArtifactCompletedEvent</b> of its transport
            and the message that introduced it shows it through
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/artifactpartview">BitAiArtifactPartView</BitLink> - see the
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/artifacts">Artifacts</BitLink> scenario for the whole chat.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            <b>Security</b>: an HTML artifact is generated markup, so it is previewed in an <b>iframe</b> with
            <b>sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-popups"</b> - its scripts run, but it is denied same-origin access to the
            page, its storage and its cookies - and only once it has finished streaming. An SVG is shown through an <b>img</b>,
            which never runs a script inside it.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            <b>Accessibility</b>: the panel is a <b>section</b> named after the artifact, its body says <b>aria-busy</b> while it
            streams, the full-screen toggle says <b>aria-pressed</b>, and Escape collapses the full-screen panel - claimed, so
            a dialog the panel sits in does not close on the same press.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Give it an <b>Artifact</b>. A <b>BitAiArtifact</b> of the <b>Code</b> kind is shown as highlighted code in the
                language its <b>Language</b> names, under a header with its title, copy, download and the full-screen toggle.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="programArtifact" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Documents" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                A <b>Document</b> is prose written in Markdown, rendered as it streams - headings, lists, tables and code - and
                downloaded as a .md file.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="documentArtifact" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="HTML pages" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                An <b>Html</b> artifact is a self-contained page, previewed in a sandboxed frame once it is complete (while it
                streams it is shown as its code). The page below runs its own script - click its button - but cannot reach the
                page it sits in. The frame keeps a white background in a dark theme, the page a generated document expects.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="pageArtifact" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="JSON & tables" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                A <b>Json</b> artifact is shown as a collapsible tree. A <b>Table</b> written as CSV - or TSV, when its
                <b>Language</b> is <b>tsv</b> - is shown as a table whose first row is the header, quoted fields and all; one
                written as a JSON array of objects becomes a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/datatable">BitAiDataTable</BitLink>.
                Each downloads in its own format.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="jsonArtifact" />
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="csvArtifact" />
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="tsvArtifact" />
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="jsonTableArtifact" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Images" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                An <b>Image</b> is shown from its <b>Url</b> (or a data URL as its content), named by its title. An <b>Svg</b>
                written as markup is shown as an image too, so a script inside it never runs, and downloads as an .svg file.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="imageArtifact" />
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="svgArtifact" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Other kinds" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>BitAiArtifactKind</b> is a string-backed value rather than an enum, so a transport may send kinds the package
                does not know. A <b>Chart</b>, a <b>Diagram</b> or a kind of the app's own (<b>new BitAiArtifactKind("mermaid")</b>)
                has no built-in rendering and is shown as its code, until the app registers a renderer for it with
                <b>RegisterArtifact</b> - which then draws the body, inside an error boundary, with a <b>BitAiArtifactContext</b>
                that also says whether the panel is full screen.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="chartArtifact" />
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="mermaidArtifact" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Streaming" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example7CodeFiles" Id="example7">
            <div>
                The panel follows the artifact as it streams - re-rendering at most once per render interval of the AI options,
                with loading dots in its header and its body marked busy. The scripted transport here writes a README into an
                artifact on the first turn and revises the same artifact on the next one, so the second press adds version 2
                and the version selector appears.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="WriteReadmeAsync" Disabled="readmeSession.IsGenerating">@(ReadmeArtifact is null ? "Write a README" : "Revise it")</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            @if (ReadmeArtifact is { } readme)
            {
                <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="readme" />
            }
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Versions" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                <b>BeginRevision</b> starts a new version and keeps the last one in <b>Versions</b>, a history of
                <b>BitAiArtifactVersion</b>s with an optional summary of what changed; <b>Complete</b> commits it. The selector
                lists every version, and the panel keeps showing the one picked while newer ones arrive. <b>InitialVersion</b>
                opens a panel at an older version - the one a particular turn produced - and <b>Restore</b> brings an old version
                back as a new one.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="RestoreFirstVersion">Restore version 1</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="pricingArtifact" />
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="pricingArtifact" InitialVersion="1" />
            <br />
            <ul>
                @foreach (var version in pricingArtifact.Versions)
                {
                    <li>Version @version.Version: @version.Summary</li>
                }
            </ul>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Copy, download & full screen" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                Copy and download act on the version on screen. The file is named after the title, with <b>-v1</b> and so on for
                an older version, and takes the extension and type of its kind and language: .md, .html, .json, .csv, .tsv, .svg,
                or one of some twenty languages (.cs, .ts, .py, ...) for code. The full-screen toggle lays the same panel over the
                page - a running page in its frame is never restarted by it - and Escape or a click on the overlay brings it back.
                A <b>Disabled</b> panel keeps copy and turns the version selector, the download and the full-screen toggle off.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="pageArtifact" />
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="pricingArtifact" Disabled />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> each part (the version selector,
                the buttons and the loading dots take the class-styles of BitDropdown, BitButton and BitLoading). The public
                <b>--bit-AiArtifactView-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table) inherit, so set on <b>:root</b>, a container
                or a panel's <b>Style</b> they re-skin every panel below - a panel a registered renderer nests inside it too.
            </div>
            <br />
            @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
            <div>
                <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="programArtifact"
                                   Style="max-width: 32rem"
                                   Styles="@(new() { Header = "background-color: #1e1e2e; color: #cdd6f4", Icon = "color: #89b4fa", Title = "font-family: monospace" })" />
                <br />
                <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="documentArtifact" Classes="@(new() { Panel = "custom-panel", Header = "custom-header" })" />
                <br />
                <div style="--bit-AiArtifactView-radius: 1rem; --bit-AiArtifactView-border-color: #d2691e; --bit-AiArtifactView-header-background: #d2691e1a; --bit-AiArtifactView-title-font-size: 1rem; --bit-AiArtifactView-max-height: 10rem">
                    <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="documentArtifact" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the header and the body read from the right, whether the direction comes from <b>Dir</b>
                or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around the panel.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiArtifactView Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Artifact="persianArtifact" />
            <br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitAiArtifactView Artifact="persianArtifact" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
